1
00:00:01,420 --> 00:02:30,930
Part 5 stopped on this same song. This sitting opens on Surdas. Why, Yashoda Maiya, have you frightened the little Kanhaiya? Mohan is our brother. How much yogurt has he drunk? [Surdas, Sursagar, raga Sorath] Barau means the small Kanhaiya. This is our dear brother. How much of your yogurt did he drink, how much butter did he eat, that you bound him? I was not at home, so you struck him with the stick, sar-sar. He was not struck, but seeing Krishna weep, Balarama says, she must have struck him. I was not in the house. You gave the stick, and you bound the ornaments on the arms. How can I live, seeing it? If I had been there and seen you strike, what would I have done then, do it now. If I had seen the stick moving and the hands being bound, I could not live. Seeing him bound, how are you living? Krishna is this dear to me. Gopal is dear to everyone, and upon him you have struck.

2
00:02:31,890 --> 00:04:58,280
Saying this much, Balarama is coming into his own form. The Lord's opulence appears sometimes, but in Braj it stays pressed down. You do not know that Krishna is greater than even us. He is small, so what? You do not know his opulence. In anger Balarama is saying, he is greater than us. If it had been anyone else, other than the mother, that person would have known. If someone else had bound him and struck him in this way, that person would have known the fruit of the act. Now the mother has bound him, so what can I do? She who gave birth, who fed and raised, can also strike. That is her right. But he is cutting out his own greatness, that I would have done this. How would that person have gone on living, if he had touched with a finger? You have used the stick, you have bound him. If it had been anyone other than you, and he had looked at Krishna after touching him with a finger, what would I have done? Still he is saying it as one who is poor.

3
00:04:58,280 --> 00:07:37,370
Then the song goes on. Balabira is standing bound, tears falling from the eyes. Hari, is yogurt, milk, and ghee dearer to you? Yashoda has bound the brother of Balavira. Krishna is already weeping, and Balarama has begun to weep. There is anger, and there is grief. Ah, Yashoda, some gopi says. Balarama is weeping, Krishna is weeping. So Yashoda, yogurt, ghee, and milk have become dearer to you than this child. A little was wasted, and the two brothers are standing in this poor condition. Balarama is wiping Krishna's tears. Then Surdas asks, as long as my life remains, birth after birth, let this image of Krishna and Balarama stay written in my heart. Sur, Shyama, the holder of the mountain, the holder of the earth, Haladhara: let this image stay steady while I live. [Surdas, Sursagar] This binding to the mortar is very dear in the hearts of the devotees. Balarama weeping and Krishna weeping, in a helpless condition: let this image live in our heart, birth after birth.

4
00:07:39,290 --> 00:10:21,150
After this Mother Yashoda is doing her own work. Balarama does not have the courage to untie him. He is wiping the eyes, full of tears. The same person, one portion of whom burns the three worlds to ash at the dissolution, and there is one rope. It has become plain before everyone: Mother bound him, so only she can untie him. No one else can. After some time the Lord signalled Sri Balarama, go now. The deliverance of these two is done. A lila that happened later is also told earlier. Each person has a taste. I had already said that the yamala-arjuna trees fell, those two were delivered, and after the prayer they left by the Lord's order. The lila before that I am telling here in the words of Surdas. Sursagar is a very large book. He sang the lilas of the Lord. There are his poems on all the lilas. We could not tell them in order. People of Rajasthan eat the papad at the end of the meal, and people of Gujarat from the beginning. Someone eats the sweet at the start. People of Bengal and Bihar eat the sweet at the end. If the sweet is missing, a lack remains. However it is, the lila happened in this way.

5
00:10:23,400 --> 00:14:20,300
When Nalakuvara and Manigriva had gone, then the sound of the trees falling happened. When the sound happened is the question. If the sound had come at once, everyone would have come running. The prayer of Nalakuvara and Manigriva would have remained unfinished. There would have been no talk with the Lord. That lila would have been obstructed. So the Lord, who regulates all of nature, holding back one sound, what great matter is that? The sound happened later. The telling begins there. Shukadeva said: the cowherds headed by Nanda, hearing the sound of the two trees falling, went there, O best of the Kurus, afraid of a thunderbolt. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.1] O Maharaj Parikshit, when the cowherds headed by Nanda heard the roar of those two trees falling, they set out that way at once. From far away they heard the roar first. They had not seen the sight. O best of the Kurus, hearing the sound, they came there. Nirghata means thunder when there is no cloud, a fall of the thunderbolt. This is counted a very great ill omen. No cloud in the sky, and still a thunderbolt, a crash: one should understand that a very heavy misfortune is coming. When the Lord left this earth, misfortunes of this kind had begun. Here there was no nirghata. The trees had fallen. But it was so frightening that everyone took it to be a thunderbolt. They became very afraid of that sound, and they went toward Braj. When the sound came, after Nalakuvara and Manigriva had left, Mother Yashoda became completely unconscious, in anxiety for her son. And the cowherds came running.

6
00:14:22,440 --> 00:18:20,080
There they saw the twin arjuna trees fallen on the ground. They were bewildered, not knowing the cause of the fall, though the fall itself was in sight. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.2] Coming to that place, they saw that the yamala-arjuna had fallen on the earth. They had not fallen by breaking. The whole root had come out. Whoever came asked, how did it fall, how was the tree uprooted? On all sides people began to talk among themselves. The heart began to pound. Some demon threw it down. There is no storm, no rain, no elephants moving here. Someone began to look carefully at the root: were there termites, did they fall by themselves from being old? There were no termites. A termite bound in a mortar threw them down: what termite would be there? Have the roots slipped, or what has happened? As the cause was not being found, the anxiety grew. Babhramuh. They fell into confusion. The cause of the fall was visible. The mortar Krishna had dragged had left a mark on the ground, up to the trees, and after the trees the mark continued. A little intelligence, and they would have understood that it fell from this. The cause of the fall is in truth the mortar. Still they fell into confusion. A great outcry had risen. Two trees falling suddenly beside the house is an ill omen. They fell without a storm, without a thunderbolt, without an elephant. So the conclusion was, some ghost, some demon is throwing them down, and has thrown them on the child. This was thrown to kill Krishna. Everyone is arriving at this decision.

7
00:18:20,440 --> 00:23:05,680
Then the attention went, ah, the child is here. He is bound. Nanda Baba will untie him. The child, bound by the rope, dragging the mortar. Whose work is this, from where is this wonder, a calamity: so they were distressed. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.3] They saw the trees fallen, and there between the two trees, the child. When the cowherds arrived, he too was performing the lila of running from fear. Oh father, the trees have fallen, so he was running, dragging the mortar in a hurry. That is why the word vikarshantam is used. He is bound by the rope, and he is dragging. It is this plain. The cause of the fall is marked. Still, who did this wonder, from where is this calamity. There was no calamity, but a tree falling quietly without a storm, and the fear of a calamity arose. All were overwhelmed. How did it fall. Who was here. Someone must have seen it falling. Some demon shook it and threw it down. Has anyone seen. They asked, and it came out that some children were here. The children too were astonished. Their breath was racing. They too were afraid. The boys said: by him. The mortar had gone crosswise. Dragging it, he went between, and we also saw two men. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.4] The verb was not coming out. Anena, by him. The breath is racing, fear is there. People ask, anena, he threw it down. This child threw it down. What are you saying. Then half a sentence. The mortar had gone crosswise. Madhya-gena, he went between. Vikarshata, he was dragging this mortar. They wanted to say, Krishna threw them down, but they are not using the verb patitah. Only this much: he was dragging the mortar, he went between, by him the trees fell. Their speech is stopping, because these children also had not arrived at that decision. What they had seen, that is what they are speaking.

8
00:23:09,820 --> 00:26:35,070
When people heard this, everyone became quiet. They forbade the children, stay quiet, do not make a noise. The children are speaking, hear what the cause is. Then they began to make it, ah, do not ask the children, it is useless, find out what the cause is. Sanatana Goswami writes on this point that the speech of children is sweeter than nectar, and more authoritative than the Veda. In children there is no flattery, no greed. In scripture, to press a point, even arthavada comes in. Children do not speak all that. They speak the plain thing that is to be spoken. So their words are more authoritative than the Veda, and sweeter than nectar. Shruti gives comfort to the ear, and shruti also means the Veda. But words like these are being spoken, and the people who did not see how the trees fell are guessing. This is the condition of the world. Those who have understood Krishna's lila say it. Then people say, no, all this is imagination. They write books by guessing, they say here and there, the trees fell like this. The children have seen. There, not one person accepted the children's word. The children were saying only this much: we saw, he went between, he was dragging the mortar, and purushau api acakshmahi. We also saw two men. Two more persons. They were scolded, what has happened to you, if you saw them, where did they go. You are seeing a dream. The mind is already troubled, and on top of that, two men. The one who speaks the truth is being cut. Acakshmahi means, we saw it with the eye, directly.

9
00:26:36,290 --> 00:32:21,510
If it had been accepted, the doubt would have left at that very time. Brother, leave it, all right, he threw them down. Upon whom is the Lord's mercy. At which times does the strength increase. As at the deliverance of Putana, or at the time of Trinavarta, or at the time of Shakatasura, he did it in the same way. And before this also, Mother Yashoda had seen the universal form, in Krishna's mouth. So some accepted something. Later, if there had been faith in the children's word, the doubt would have gone. But they never accept it. That poor condition of Krishna, Krishna's child's lila, that sweet lila. He has thrown down the tree, we have also seen the men. They did not take what was said. They did not accept it. It cannot be, this uprooting of the two trees by that child. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.5] The uprooting of two trees by a child is not possible at all. A thousand elephants could not uproot these trees, and a small child uproots them: this can never happen. They accepted, all right, he is dragging the mortar, he went into the tree, but he did not throw these trees down. When the Lord's lilas are told in society, the Bhagavatam describes that Sri Krishna lifted the mountain, at the time of the Indra worship, and protected the people of Braj. [Srimad-Bhagavatam, Tenth Canto, the Govardhana lila] Hearing this lila, those who have a very great feeling for the Lord say, he will not be able to lift it. Like the people of Braj. From feeling they say, he cannot lift it. And those who do not understand the Lord's meaning at all say, all these are things that have been heard. Where is a child of seven years, and where is a mountain seven kos high, held on one finger. Certainly this is some god. Have you asked someone, somewhere, who this is. What should Nanda Maharaj say. The name-giving had been secret. That day Nanda Baba said it: Garga Acharya had told us this and this. What he had heard straight from the guru's mouth, that is what he told. He did not change a word. The people of Braj were that bewildered. Bewildered means bewildered in love. All right, it is Narayana's mercy. Garga Acharya had said, protect this child. And he had said before that those who love him, this child will protect them. Before also he will do the work of kings, he will flourish. But the secret must not be opened, so at the end he said, Nanda Baba, you protect your own child. And he had said before that in qualities he is equal to Narayana. When the gopas heard the last sentence, they are full of vatsalya, full of affection. Toward the Lord the intelligence that he is God does not come. And this itself is the success of life. Such true love for the Lord, such attachment. This they did not accept.

10
00:32:24,800 --> 00:37:15,510
A whole mass of earth had been torn up from under them. So how can a child uproot it. All were logicians. Logician here does not mean the logician of today. They were looking by this reasoning: he is a child, this is his condition, he was caught in a theft, he has been beaten, he is bound. A child has no strength against a vast tree. All are speaking from their own logic, from the mind's guess. Sridhara Swami says the logicians did not accept it. The meaning of the logic is, they were all considering, this cannot be, the uprooting of two trees by one child is not possible. But some were of doubtful mind. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.5] Jiva Goswami says there were some brahmanas in Braj. They too were joined with the feeling of vatsalya. But the later matters, the account of Putana, of Trinavarta, of Shakatasura, began to be remembered. And Mother Yashoda had just said, in his mouth I saw the whole world. It is not known what has happened to the child, please do a sweeping and a blowing, please treat him. So all the brahmanas, please treat him, speaking the Lord's name. When the whole universe was seen in the mouth, was this some illness of Krishna, that Mother Yashoda had a treatment done. They gave a blessing. So these brahmanas, remembering the earlier lilas, became somewhat doubtful in mind. It may be that he himself threw them down. They made no decision. They stayed in the middle. Yes, the children also were saying it, and guessing from the earlier events, they fell into doubt. They did not accept that Krishna had thrown them down. Some doubt happened. His own son, dragging the mortar, bound by the rope. Nanda, seeing him, released him with a smiling face. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.6] Their vatsalya, their affection, increased further. The child was saved, this happiness was there. Nanda Maharaj became very happy. He began to laugh. Because he saw, the child is very healthy. He saw the strength, he is dragging the mortar. The face opened, my child is free of illness. The trees also fell, and it had no effect on him. They had not yet seen him dragging a heavy thing like this. Every day he was walking, dragging stones this heavy. And it was the first time they had seen him bound in this way. It became known who had bound him, so the laughter was coming. And he was also laughing so that the child, seeing a laughing face, would be pleased. He should not think, Mummy bound me, so Papa has come and will start beating. It happens, does it not. The father comes into the house, and the mother has given a child a little punishment, and he asks what wrong was done, and some fathers give one more slap. First the mother has beaten. So Nanda Maharaj untied him with a smiling mouth. He untied the rope from Krishna's waist, threw it, and lifted him into the lap. Joy spread. The child was very happy.

11
00:37:16,240 --> 00:40:38,290
He is telling the other lilas again, how the Lord stayed under the people of Braj, and gave them happiness according to their wish. Encouraged by the gopis, the Lord danced like a child. Sometimes he sang aloud, bewildered, under their control, like a wooden doll. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.7] The gopis also, who had the feeling of vatsalya, encouraged the child. Lala, if you will show this dance, I will give you a big londa of butter. You understand londa. Fresh butter taken out, firm, a round, set on the hand: that is called a londa. Now it is not even to be seen, but before, it was there to eat. Taking a londa of butter in the hand, the gopi would say, Krishna Lala, if you dance I will give this. Then he begins to dance, stepping and stepping. Whatever they want done, Krishna is ready, in any house. It was not that he would sing and dance, and then they would give something to eat. They make a condition like this in order to see his singing and dancing, and it seemed that the Lord is singing and dancing from the greed of eating. Exactly like a child. Sometimes he danced, sometimes he sang in a high voice, sometimes he performed the lila of one completely bewildered, as if he had no knowledge of place and time. They wanted him to dance, he danced. They wanted him to sing, he sang.

12
00:40:38,290 --> 00:44:34,210
Even so far that sometimes they gave an order, son, bring it. He brought it. Bring the wooden seat. The pidha. A seat made of wood, on which people sit at the time of eating, is called a pidha. Nanda Maharaj, or any person of Braj, bring the pidha. Their hope was, the boy will start lifting weight from now, then strength will grow in his arm, he will stay healthy. Children should be given work, exercise, every task, then the body stays right. Sometimes he brought the pidha. It would not lift, and he put in a great effort. And the paduka. Sometimes he brought the wooden sandals, or the shoes. The Lord, the husband of Lakshmi, by the order of the people of Braj, brought their shoes. The question comes, why did they tell him to bring shoes. The pidha and the rest were for growing strength. Why for bringing shoes. They would say, son, bring the shoes. He would go running and bring a shoe. Of the pair, he brought one at a time. There was not the intelligence to pick up both and bring them. This lila kept happening. And sometimes he threw his arms. I have become very strong, I can fight with someone. He increases the affection of his own people of Braj, of his own kin. To those who worship the Lord with the feeling that he is God, who are his servants, this was to be shown: I am under the control of loving devotion. Those who worship him taking him as the master are called tad-vidam. Those who know the truth of the Lord, who know that Krishna is God, that the living being is his eternal servant, and who worship Krishna as God in the mood of a servant. To those people the Lord wanted to show: you should worship me taking me as a son, or taking me as a friend. Then I stay in the control of such devotees. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.8-9] When they do this lila, those who know the truth of the Lord understand, the Lord can be this easy for us, he can become our son.

13
00:44:37,090 --> 00:49:21,840
The Lord became pleased. There was love. Even for a Bhil woman living in the forest, far from Braj, the Lord had as much love as for Mother Yashoda. Any person of Braj, whether a woman, a man, or other living beings, the Lord gave them great happiness. Now the katha of the Bhil woman. There was a Bhil woman living in the forest. Picking fruit, small fruits such as pilu, or piyar, chironji, she filled a basket and brought them to sell. O you who want fruit, buy the fruits. Take fruit, buy fruit. When she was passing Nanda Baba's house and said, buy fruit, the moment he heard it Achyuta ran quickly, to take fruit. He went first. He went with grain in the hand. Before this Krishna had seen that Mother Yashoda, when she bought some fruit, went with grain from the house. Even today this thing is in the village. Someone comes to the village to sell something, and the woman goes with grain. He went with grain in the hand. Wheat, or rice, whatever it was, he picked it up quickly and ran. He gives every fruit to everyone. Dharma, artha, kama, moksha, heaven, liberation, everything is in his hand. When the Lord is worshiped by sacrifices, the Lord gives heaven. Learned people ask for heaven, and I give them heaven. Those who want Vaikuntha, who want my abode, I give the abode. And in this world also, those who want wealth, fame, their own people, he gives them everything. But that same Lord, who is the giver of every fruit, today became one who asks for fruit, and to pay the price of the fruit he picked up grain in the joined palms and went. In walking he was a child. He was walking, stepping and stumbling. He does not even know how to close the palms properly. So by the time he reached that Bhil woman, all the grain in the hand had fallen. Wherever he put a foot, he was spilling still more, shaking. This too is an uncommon lila. All the grain fell. The Lord who could not even keep the grain safe in his own hand. Vishvanatha Chakravarti writes that two or four grains were stuck, only in the fingers. Both hands had become empty of grain.

14
00:49:23,280 --> 00:53:35,230
Then that Bhil woman filled the child's hands with fruits, pilu or chironji and the rest, taking them from her basket. She wanted to give more, but only that much could be held. The poor woman did not wait for the grain to arrive and then give. The fruit seller, his two hands from which the grain had fallen, she filled with fruits, and the fruit basket was filled with jewels. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.11] The child was so beautiful, so attractive, so bright, that a feeling of vatsalya like Mother Yashoda's came into her mind. She had brought very beautiful fruit. She knew that Nanda Baba has one boy, and it is to be taken and given to him. It is the family of the king. If she had said, I will give it later, they would have felt shy. So she was saying, buy the fruit. She filled the Lord's empty hands with fruit. Where she gave fruit into the Lord's hands, her basket filled with jewels. With gold, with diamonds. People who actually learn to give to the Lord can never have a lack. This should be learned in life. Whatever one has, it should be offered to the Lord. The Lord worries for us more than we do: what does this one need. One joined-palm of fruit was not a great gift from the Lord's side. But from the side of smallness, that person is qualified for the happiness of service. For this the Lord filled that basket. By one act he is saying both. She filled the Lord's empty hands with a little, and the Lord filled the basket with jewels. The cause was that she gave to the Lord, therefore the jewels. It touches by feeling. Someone gives even one coin, gives even a tulasi leaf, and he remembers that thing. Great souls speak in this way. Once the Lord, at Maharaj Yudhishthira's, is cutting some vegetable in the kitchen. The knife struck his finger. The other people started, a leaf should be brought, cloth should be brought to bind it, blood is coming out. Right there Queen Draupadi sees it. The finger is cut, there is no time to arrange for someone. Then they say that the Lord later increased that same gift into crores of meters of sari. The Lord remembers everything. In this way the Lord is in truth everyone's. Not only for Yashoda and the queens of Braj. He is for all living beings. The living being who makes this relation solid is blessed. From that day she left the selling. And she brought fruit only for Krishna. She would go to the forest, pick, with great care, whichever had come very sweet, that alone she would pick and bring. The Lord would eat what she had brought.

15
00:53:37,380 --> 00:57:31,380
It was the talk of one day. The acharyas say, the day after the day on which the yamala-arjuna were delivered. Krishna, who had gone to the riverbank, the breaker of the arjunas, Rohini Devi then called, and Rama, playing intently with the boys. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.12] Krishna was playing with the children on the bank of the Yamuna, at that pleasant place where the sand is seen, where the field is. Many kinds of play. Walking on smooth ground, putting on mud. Maharaj said, the time has gone, where are the children, go, call them. So Yashoda sends Rohini, go and bring them. Rohini goes and speaks. In the verse there is bhagnarjunam. The book's meaning is, after the breaking of the yamala-arjuna, Rohini Devi called Krishna, who had gone to the riverbank, and Rama, who were playing with deep absorption among the boys. Maharaj reads this word as the call. The first name she spoke was this, O Bhagnarjuna. O breaker of the arjuna trees, come this way. O Rama, come this way, come. Your prasadam, the meal, is ready. It has become very late. But Krishna did not come. Then Rohini came and said to Yashoda, sister, you go, you bring them. Yashoda was sent. Rohini sent Yashoda, who is affectionate to the sons. Yashoda's affection for Sri Balarama and for Krishna. And they are playing with the elder brother. A long hour has passed. So Yashoda began to call from far away. From love of the son the breast flowed. The milk came down. If one calls from far, they can come. Going near and saying come, they did not come. They did not come into the hand. What Maharaj was asking from the morning, what had Rohini said, how had she called. She had said, I called him Bhagnarjuna. And on being called Bhagnarjuna he did not come. Maharaj says, the Lord remembered that ahead a friend will be made, Arjuna, and I hold Arjuna very dear. Why is she speaking a name like this. So he did not come for that name. This is his explanation. The book's word, bhagna-arjuna, belongs to those trees.

16
00:57:32,920 --> 01:01:02,160
But she is Mother Yashoda, so they say it. Krishna, Krishna, lotus-eyed one, dear child, come, drink the breast. Enough of playing. You are worn by hunger, tired from play, my son. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.15] O Krishna, O Krishna. This name is the dearest to Krishna. By this name he certainly comes. Krishna, Krishna. And after that, aravindaksha, lotus-eyed. Dear child, you are very beautiful. You have played enough. Leave it. You have become thin from hunger. You have become tired from playing, and you are looking hungry. So come, come, drink the breast. Krishna was not coming. After this, am I not good. O Rama, come quickly, dear child, with your younger brother, joy of the family. You ate in the morning. You ought to eat. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.16] O Rama, O dear son, come quickly. Come with your younger brother. Krishna wanted to hear this, O Krishna, come with your elder brother. One is coming as the helper, one is the chief who is to come. It is inside both, is it not. So she says to Balarama, you come with your younger brother. And O joy of the family, O increaser of the family's joy. Balarama, you have been born excellent in this family, so four moons have been added to the family. You are very good. On this, as if Krishna should think, you called him the joy of the family, so have I taken birth as one who puts a stain on the family. By producing envy in Krishna, she is calling him quickly. O Rama, O dear son, come with your younger brother. You come, and bring him. Then Krishna became fair. Now he became the chief. And kula-nandana, the increaser of the family's joy, was also said. They had eaten in the early morning. In the morning they had taken food once. Now it is midday. Come. You should certainly eat as one eats properly. You are hungry. Come, eat.

17
01:01:02,450 --> 01:05:16,990
Nanda Maharaj is waiting for the meal. Do not give him pain either. Let him also eat. Come quickly. Without you they will not eat. Come, do what is dear to us both. She says to the other children, children, you also go to your own homes, because your mothers and fathers will also be saying, come, come. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.17] I saw that these two are not coming yet, so she sent the children away. When it was said, come to eat, still Krishna is not coming. Then Mother Yashoda says further. Your body is grey with dust, son, come and bathe. Today is the star of your birth. Being pure, give cows to the brahmanas. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.18] Today is your birth star. Come, bathe. Give cows in charity to the brahmanas. Become pure and see. And those with you, whom Mother Yashoda had brought, their ornaments also: the mother has put on such beautiful cloth, has done the adornment. The Lord keeps his attention on giving. It is to be given. In truth this feeling is very good, the feeling of giving. Today is your birth star, come, give cows to the brahmanas. From the beginning, the feeling of giving should be raised in children. Whatever one's own condition, to the poor, to the sadhu, to the Lord, the habit of giving should be put into one's children. Mother Yashoda says to her son, come, bathe. The cause is that the moment he hears this, the child will come. From the beginning Krishna's taste is in giving. And our culture is that when we give a thing, to some brahmana, to a sadhu, or to a poor person, we give it from the child's hand. Give the child the coin, the grain, the letter, offer obeisance and give. This is a very beautiful thought. The training of giving has been put into the children. For the sake of saying this, the Lord came. Sri Balarama also came to Mother Yashoda.

18
01:05:34,990 --> 01:09:30,510
In this way Yashoda, her intelligence bound by affection, considered him who is the crown of all that is endless to be her son, O King. Taking the infallible one by the hand, together with Rama, she brought him to her own courtyard and did what makes a child shine. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.20] In this way Yashoda considers Krishna only her own son. The intelligence is bound by affection. He is her own son, he is unknowing. He is to be fed on time, given drink, bathed, adorned. This alone is the intelligence. And whereas Krishna is the crown of everything without remainder. He is the greatest Lord, the Lord of all. But even such a Lord, considering him a son. Born from my body, he is my boy, considering him so. We consider the Lord. What has been considered in society, until now the same, glory, O Lord of the universe. [Jayadeva, Dashavatara] This is what we have considered. The Lord is the Lord of the universe, and if we offer him obeisance all the wealth will come into the house, the sin will end. Each person has a conviction about Krishna. But Yashoda's conviction was this: he is my son, he is unknowing, he is to be raised, he is to be protected. Matva, considering, this word is of great weight. People who consider Lord Krishna to be God are not doing wrong either. The Lord is God. He is the Supreme Lord. I stay the dearest, I stay ahead. Then taking by the hand, with Rama. Taking the hand of both sons, one with the left hand and the other with the right. And whatever else was to be done for the child's growing, for his happiness, Mother Yashoda did it. This was a daily lila, calling in this way, feeding, bathing, adorning. But considering Krishna a son. Not considering him the Supreme Lord. Sri Krishna, who is the crown of all without remainder, take him as your own son. By these kathas those feelings should be nourished. Toward Krishna, the feeling of a parent, the feeling of a friend, the feeling of sweetness.

19
01:09:34,480 --> 01:14:24,970
Om namo bhagavate vasudevaya. Shukadeva said: the elder cowherds, having experienced great calamities in Brihadvana, Nanda and the others assembled and took counsel on the work of Vraja. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.21] After the day of the binding to the mortar, the Indra sacrifice was about to begin. The festival of the worship of Indra went on for many days. So all the gopas stayed busy in it. When that lila was described, that Krishna played with his friends on the bank of the Yamuna, and Mother Yashoda called them and brought them, that passage had happened. Then, for about seven or eight days after Diwali, the gopas stayed busy in the worship of Indra. When the worship of Indra ended, the gopas held an ishta-goshti. Ishta means near, one's own person. A goshti happens when people gather in one place and consider with one another. The meaning of ishta-goshti is a meeting of people of one heart, for some particular work. The chief among the gopas was Nanda Maharaj. It did not happen regularly. Having experienced great calamities in Brihadvana. Brihadvana, or Mahavana. In Mahavana many great calamities were coming. This point came into the minds of the gopas, that the cause is in the place. So many troubles are coming in this place. Especially, the life of everyone is Krishna, and the troubles were coming upon Krishna alone. Whenever a danger came, as when Putana came, the attempt to kill Krishna. The cart fell, the wheel of the cart, made completely unsteady, overturned. No earthquake, no storm, nothing. It fell upon the child alone. But the Lord saved him. The thinking of these people did not work. After that Trinavarta snatched him and took him, very high in the sky. He dashed the child from above. By the mercy of Lord Vishnu. And just now two heavy trees fell. The boy stayed between them, and still he did not die. All this they will consider in the meeting. Again and again they were experiencing great calamities of this kind. All had become very worried, had become afraid. How will the child be protected.

20
01:14:26,850 --> 01:18:31,180
The meaning of protecting Krishna was this itself. If Krishna stays well, the whole circle of Braj will stay well, will stay healthy, will stay happy. Without Krishna there is no imagining Braj. Krishna alone is the life of Braj. Of the people of Braj, of the cows, of all. So this assembly that was called was in truth called for Krishna's safety. Sanatana Goswami writes that the meaning of the work of Vraja is to do Krishna's good. Krishna's protection is first. They want to keep him saved. And Garga Acharya had said to Nanda Maharaj, protect him, protect this child. Because of the thickness of the affection, this emergency meeting had been called. Many disturbances were coming. So the consideration was, what should be done so that now no calamity comes, every danger goes away, and this child named Krishna stays in happiness. That itself is called the work of Vraja. It should be remembered that the gopas had no meeting hall for holding an assembly. They were sitting under a tree. The entrance of children and of women was not there. What would they go to a meeting for. But the subject of this meeting was Krishna. Krishna is to be protected. Sanatana Goswami writes that when Upananda will speak, it seems that Krishna is there. As the great elder gopas are holding the meeting. The women were not in it either. Only the elder cowherds were there. People of great experience, long-lived, and knowing policy. They were taking counsel among themselves. Then Krishna is there. Let him use the word balakah, this child. Jiva Goswami says that pointing with his finger, Upananda speaks, this child. He is a child just now, safe, by the Lord's mercy. So everyone's mind is soft, is moist, filled with affection for the child, with affection for Krishna. And the assembly is happening for Krishna's protection. How great an assembly it is. All the elder cowherds, filled with the feeling of vatsalya. Krishna should be protected. This is the work of Vraja. So they began to take counsel among themselves, how to do it, how to protect.

21
01:18:37,480 --> 01:23:45,270
They call him Upananda also, Upananda. All of these did the work of a minister of Nanda Maharaj. In any difficult condition Nanda Maharaj acted according to their counsel. So Nanda Maharaj asked. Then Upananda Maharaj spoke. Before the speaking, Shukadeva is giving a little introduction of him. There a cowherd named Upananda spoke, greater in knowledge and in age, knowing the truth of place, time, and purpose, doing what is dear to Rama and Krishna. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.22] Upananda Maharaj was the eldest in knowledge and in age. In that assembly he was old in knowledge and old in years. So he was given the turn. Let him speak, because he knows the truth of place, time, and purpose. What should be done in which country, what should be done at which time, what purpose is accomplished. If one's own purpose is to be accomplished, in which place one should live, and at which time the dealing should be done. Keeping the knowledge of place and time is very necessary. Upananda Maharaj was very skilled. And the greatest point was that he did what is dear to Rama and Krishna. He was the supreme well-wisher of Sri Balarama and Krishna, the one who does what is dear. In his life this alone was the work, to keep Krishna and Balarama happy, to keep them pleased. He had a very great intelligence, he had the knowledge of place and time, and he was greater in age also, but he was greater in affection also. Supreme affection for Sri Krishna, and for Sri Balarama. His dear ones were Krishna. Here there is also this hope, that in order to do what is dear to Rama and Krishna, to give them happiness, to do their good, he spoke. Or from his own nature. That place is now to be left. For the gopas, from one view, leaving was not a very great matter. Because in the rains Nanda Maharaj's place of living was Nandishvara, Nandagaon. From there they had already come, here into Brihadvana. It is seen even today: for his own lila the Lord chose the most suitable community and place, the gopas and the circle of Braj. Here too there is a very great point. The Lord made the gopa community itself the vessel of his lila, and he chose this land of the circle of Braj. One question comes: was this already there, and the Lord chose it, or did the Lord send all this from Goloka. The fact is that Goloka is not different from Vrindavan. The Lord has manifested all of it from beginningless time. They are not some ordinary gopa community. In truth they are all the Lord's eternal associates. They have come here, and with them the Lord has done the lila. At this place also Upananda Maharaj spoke in this way. It should be kept in mind that when he speaks, in his sight there is only the talk of protecting Krishna and Balarama, of doing their good.

22
01:24:06,010 --> 01:26:30,810
The first thing he said: now we should leave this place. How could all the people accept this point. This is our own house. The house is filled with a great deal of goods. There are children, there are women, there are cows, spread out, very far. Taking all the goods, they should go to a new place. A house will have to be built there. How much anxiety there is. If even one place, even the door, is to be left, this is a trouble. But in order to do what is dear to the Lord, in order to do Krishna's good, let the village be uprooted, whatever it may be. In truth it is the love of the gopas. Whatever one's own pleasant house is, it is to be left in order to save Krishna. We who wish the good of Gokula should rise up from here. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.23] We should move on from here, we should get up. We are the well-wishers of Gokula. We want the good of the cows, so we should leave this place. In the good of Gokula the cows will stay well, and then Krishna will be raised. And the fact was that from living a long time in Brihadvana, the grass there was nearly finished, and there was a great crowding. From every side the gopas had come, bringing their own cows. When the Lord came there, moved by an unknown attraction, all the gopas had come into Brihadvana bringing their cows. So the crowding happened. The good of the cows is happening. The cows even today eat their fill, drink water, and are free of illness.

23
01:26:32,320 --> 01:31:13,240
Then Upananda Maharaj says, great calamities are coming here, causes of the children's destruction. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.23] This is the real point. Balanam nasha-hetavah, for the destruction of the children. If they wish, the calamities will not come. What restriction is there on their coming. Whatever it is, the cause in this is love for Krishna alone. Balanam is speaking in the plural. But its meaning is Krishna. He is speaking in the plural because of respect. When respect is to be given to someone. If someone says, Prabhupada is coming, in the singular, that is not a sentence of respect. Prabhupada are coming, in the plural, that is in respect. Many disturbances are happening here for the destruction of the children. Hiding his own affection, he is using the plural. This is the nature of love, that it hides itself, it covers itself. He does not say, many disturbances are happening for Krishna's destruction. He says, for the destruction of the children. The greatest cause became the falling of the trees, in the holding of the meeting. So many calamities are coming here for the destruction of the children. They are coming here, in this place, continually. Great calamities. For the destruction of the children. So he is remembering all those things that have already happened. This child was somehow freed from that rakshasi. He is saying, asau balakah, this child was somehow freed from that rakshasi. Freed from Putana. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.11.24] It was said that the elder cowherds were holding a meeting. From where has this child come. There is so much respect in the mind that first Upananda is speaking in the plural, but at once he says, this child was somehow freed from Putana's attack. This is the meaning of asau balakah. Sanatana Goswami says that Upananda Maharaj had brought Krishna in his very lap, into the meeting. This may be one way, and so he says, this child somehow escaped. Or Nanda Maharaj will have been holding him in the lap. Or Krishna will not have come there. He stays so much in the memory that for him they will say, this child. Or Krishna saw that the elder cowherds are holding a meeting, and he will have come wandering by himself. The child. But Upananda Maharaj says, somehow he escaped. We had done pious work. In the previous birth, in this birth, we have done pious work, we have done the worship of the Lord. For this reason. This sitting stops here.
